{"name":"Getting Wet","hex":"#c3dae3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"getting wet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of get wet"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/getting_wet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moisture","blocks":[{"text":"Moisture is the presence of a liquid, especially water, often in trace amounts. Moisture is defined as water in the adsorbed or absorbed phase. Small amounts of water may be found, for example, in the air (humidity), in foods, and in some commercial products. Moisture also refers to the amount of water vapor present in the air. The soil also includes moisture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Spider_web_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Spider_web_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moisture","wikidata":"Q217651"},{"title":"Wetting","blocks":[{"text":"Wetting is the ability of a liquid to maintain contact with a solid surface by displacing another substance or material – either a gas, or other liquid not miscible with the wetting liquid – due to the differential strength of intermolecular interactions with the surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/A_water_droplet_DWR-coated_surface2_edit1.jpg/330px-A_water_droplet_DWR-coated_surface2_edit1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wetting","wikidata":"Q817136"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/MeltingCondensationWindow.jpg/500px-MeltingCondensationWindow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/MeltingCondensationWindow.jpg/1280px-MeltingCondensationWindow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimed